     "note": "THE 1835 GROUNDBREAKING IS NOT ANDREAS. The separately admitted June 10 Chicago Democrat notice establishes an active project before the scene; modern compilations are not the only evidence for this site. chicagology_prefire112 prints an Andreas passage and then two uncredited editorial paragraphs with no typographic break. ANDREAS says: 'Chicago did, however, have a really grand hotel as early as 1835; this was the Lake House, located on the corners of Kinzie, Rush and Michigan streets, fronting on the latter. This hotel, which was built of brick, was three stories and a basement in height, was elegantly furnished throughout, and cost its owners nearly $100,000. ... The hotel was completed and thrown open to the public in the autumn of 1836.' He gives NO groundbreaking. THE UNCREDITED PARAGRAPH says: 'ground was broken in 1835, and opened in the fall of 1836'. The same paragraph drops Andreas's basement and gives $90,000 against his nearly $100,000, so it is demonstrably not a faithful restatement of the text it sits under, and 'ground broken in 1835' must not be cited as Andreas. data/exclusions.json's lake_house_finished entry sources the 1835 groundbreaking to drloih_hotels, which is a second unfootnoted compilation saying the same thing; two compilations agreeing is not two witnesses. SO: from 1835-01-01, the first day the attested year permits and not a date anybody gives. TO 1836-12-31, a bound at the end of the year the hotel opened - Andreas's 'autumn of 1836' ends this construction state and no source dates the day. WHAT NARROWS THE MIDDLE, and it is the best anchor this record has: J. D. Bonnell, in a letter to the Chicago Times dated 15 March 1876 and quoted by Andreas, describes arriving in Chicago and walking the town the next morning - 'Passing east, toward the mouth of the river, was THE LAKE HOUSE IN COURSE OF CONSTRUCTION, east of which was the residence of Dr. Kimball.' Bonnell dates his arrival in the same letter to 'August 25, 1835'. That is eight weeks after the scene date and it is an eyewitness saying the building was visibly going up and not finished. HIS DATE IS NOT CLEAN AND THE CONFLICT IS RECORDED RATHER THAN HIDDEN: the letter opens 'My first entry into the city of Chicago was forty years ago, August 25, 1835' - forty years before 1876 is 1836 - and Andreas's own lead-in says Bonnell 'came to Chicago in 1837'. The 1837 reading is ruled out by the letter's own content, since the Lake House was open by the autumn of 1836; between 1835 and 1836 the letter's explicit date is preferred. Harriet Martineau, quoted by Andreas from June 1836, still calls it 'a new hotel then building', which is consistent with either. CURRENT EVIDENCE CORRECTION: the earlier ONLY THING wording is superseded by the separate Chicago Democrat notice of 10 June 1835, page 2 column 5, already cited under function. Its transcription-mediated reading supports an active named project before the scene but no precise groundbreaking day, roof or construction stage. The existing January 1 bound remains inferred, not an observed start date."
