-  "research_note": "LATER ADDRESS CHECK: Fergus's Directory of the City of Chicago, 1839, published in 1876, lists the Lake House at \"Rush and North Water sts\" in its Hotels section (p. 37). That line has been checked directly in the printed page image. Andreas instead names Kinzie, Rush and Michigan streets. These later street accounts remain in disagreement: this reading does not locate or describe the building site on 1 July 1835. Its existing placement and reconstructed construction stage remain unchanged.\n\nLATER COMPLETED-STATE PICTURE, NOT JULY 1835 CONSTRUCTION EVIDENCE: kellogg_blanchard_1856_old_fort_lake_house identifies the 1856 Kellogg/Blanchard print (the misleadingly named chicago_fort_dearborn_river_mouth_1830.jpg). Its labelled Lake House is the completed hotel right of the foreground fort blockhouse, partly behind a tree; the scene is approximately 1836–1849. The Images link is contextual and does not establish the height, roof or construction progress of this unfinished July 1835 shell. No camera, geometric match or grade promotion is claimed. A BUILDING SITE IS A STATE, AND THIS RECORD IS AN ARGUMENT ABOUT ONE. Everything about the finished Lake House is documented - brick, three storeys and a basement, nearly $100,000, five named backers, opened in the autumn of 1836 - and the scene-year press records an active project, but not its height, roof or detailed progress on 1 July 1835. THREE SOURCE PROBLEMS TRAVEL WITH IT AND ALL THREE ARE RECORDED ON THE ATTRIBUTES. (1) THE 1835 GROUNDBREAKING IS NOT ANDREAS. It comes from the uncredited editorial paragraph on chicagology_prefire112, which sits under a transcribed Andreas passage with no typographic break, drops Andreas's basement, and gives $90,000 where Andreas gives nearly $100,000. data/exclusions.json sources the same claim to drloih_hotels, a second unfootnoted compilation. That editorial groundbreaking date is not two independent witnesses, but the separate Chicago Democrat notice of 10 June 1835, page 2 column 5, already cited under function, does report a new house building in Kinzie's Addition, understood to be named Lake House. Its held transcription has bracketed supplies; no newspaper scan was newly inspected. The notice supports the project before the scene, not an exact groundbreaking date or progress, and the discrepancy in the cost is the proof that the paragraph is not restating the text above it. (2) THE STREET NAME IS A TRAP. Andreas twice puts the house at Kinzie, Rush and Michigan streets, fronting Michigan. North-side Michigan Street is today's East Hubbard Street, one block north of Kinzie; it is not Michigan Avenue, which did not cross the river until 1920. The gloss 'near where the Wrigley Building stands today' - on chicagology_prefire112 and repeated in docs/research/03-structures-north.md section 3.8, which also renders the street as 'Michigan (Water)' - moves the site about 150 m and across Kinzie Street, and appears to be a modern confusion of the street with the avenue. This record follows Andreas. (3) THE PROGRESS BY JULY IS THE INTERESTING GAP AND IT STAYS OPEN. The best anchor found is J. D. Bonnell's letter to the Chicago Times of 15 March 1876, quoted by Andreas, which describes walking the town the morning after arriving and seeing 'the Lake House in course of construction'. Bonnell dates that arrival to 25 August 1835 - eight weeks after the scene date - though the same letter's 'forty years ago' implies 1836 and Andreas's own lead-in says 1837. The 1837 reading is impossible on the letter's own content, since the hotel was open by the autumn of 1836. Harriet Martineau, in June 1836, still calls it 'a new hotel then building'. So the structure was demonstrably unfinished in mid-1836 and probably visibly rising in late August 1835, and this record retains one storey of roofless brickwork as a reconstructed worksite choice, with the honest range stated as zero to two storeys. Being unfinished in 1836 does not prove roof absence in July 1835. WHAT WOULD SETTLE IT: the Chicago American, whose first issue is 8 June 1835 and which would have reported a $100,000 hotel going up; the Chicago Democrat; or the Kinzie's Addition conveyances. See docs/RESEARCH/lake_house_construction.md.",
+  "research_note": "The Lake House was building before this scene: the Chicago Democrat of 10 June 1835 reports a new house building in Kinzie's Addition, understood to be named Lake House. The held newspaper reading is transcription-mediated, with bracketed supplies; no original facsimile was newly read. Andreas says the completed hotel was brick, three storeys and a basement, and opened in autumn 1836.\n\nLIBERTIES: No source dates its precise progress on 1 July. The scene chooses an incomplete, roofless working lift: stepped brick walls, seven selected work bays, open apertures and two internal timber stages. The 24 × 15 m extent, four-metre maximum, masonry thickness, smaller lifts and staging are reconstructed. Brick, timber and footing stone beside it are the existing reconstructed yard layer. No below-grade cellar is depicted. The open shell represents one bounded choice within the retained zero-to-two-storey uncertainty; that uncertainty does not require an empty site.\n\nTHE PICTURE SHOWS A LATER STATE: kellogg_blanchard_1856_old_fort_lake_house is the public-domain 1856 Kellogg/Blanchard print, misleadingly stored under an 1830 filename. Its labelled Lake House has a completed roof, cupola and three window rows in a scene of about 1836–1849. It is linked as later context and does not attest this July stage. No completed-state geometric match or July picture is claimed. The duplicate planning image is the same composition; Milwaukee's Lake House is a different hotel. The source page also carries an about-1860 completed-hotel photograph and an 1869 map, plus two later text-ad/menu images; none depicts July progress.\n\nSITING: The existing reconstructed position follows Andreas's Kinzie/Rush/Michigan account and the north-side Michigan Street reading (today Hubbard), with about twenty metres of survey uncertainty and an unresolved side-of-Rush choice. Fergus's 1839 address recalled in 1876 instead prints Rush and North Water. The source page's 1869 map places the later hotel west of Rush, contradicting the existing reconstructed east-side choice. This is recorded as a later mapped-state conflict, without asserting that the later plan measures this July worksite. That later conflict does not justify moving the July site to the modern Wrigley gloss. Bonnell's date conflict remains recorded in the research dossier. The source/date coverage and finite worksite acceptance are in docs/RESEARCH/lake-house-completion-20261110/README.md.",
