-  "research_note": "CURRENT COMPOUND INCREMENT: John Beach's separate keeper house, a continuous pointed palisade front and a low braced hung gate are represented as one source-informed continuity reconstruction. Gale1902 p51 places the house partly within the enclosure facing Randolph during an1835-framed recollection; the accompanying plate explicitly says1836. Neither source supplies a metric site plan or proves this exact July1835 state. The domestic house supersedes the older one-roof/open-square assertion. The original log-cell geometry and shallow12degree roof remain unchanged, with a1.30m southward reconstructed access setback explicitly declared in position.note. The Gale comparison still differs in exposed rear-core roof and compound proportions; Andreas shows a conflicting shallow cap and different house elevation. T-2380 stays open and no picture match or geographic camera is claimed. The verified1902 US publication supplies public-domain artwork; the separately catalogued UCR copy retains its own credit and copy-specific hold.\n\n DATED HISTORICAL SOURCE AND MODEL RATIONALES FOLLOW, preserved from the preceding record; older statements of no new placement or a sole roof are superseded by the current paragraph and phase change_note.\n\n CURRENT IMAGE EVIDENCE: The exact detailed jail drawing is verified in Edwin O. Gale’s 1902 book, illustrated by W. E. S. Trowbridge. Its actual title and copyright pages establish a public-domain publication; the earlier artwork-level hold below is superseded. The separate UCR copy is catalogued Date Created and/or Issued 19 September 1930, which still does not establish publication in 1930 or an item-specific reuse licence. Its required credit is Keystone-Mast Collection, UCR/California Museum of Photography, University of California at Riverside. Use the independently verified 1902 plate as the artwork source. The caption describes 1836, and the jail core is mostly concealed by its palisade; exact July 1835 form and component continuity remain unresolved. This correction derives no new geometry or asset from the UCR copy and changes no building form.\n\nHISTORICAL MODEL RATIONALE AND DATED SOURCE REASSESSMENTS FOLLOW.\n\nA DOCUMENTED BUILDING WITH ONE DOCUMENTED ATTRIBUTE. What is attested is that it was a jail, that it was of logs, that it went up in the fall of 1833, and that it stood at the north-west corner of the court-house square. Everything else in this record - every dimension, the roof, the wall height, the door, the bearing - is inference or invention, and the confidence view will show it as such. THE RECORD IS WORTH HAVING ANYWAY, and this is the clearest case in the parcel for the working policy behind it: on 1835-07-01 the public square carried the jail and the roofless estray pen; the brick courthouse was not erected until the fall of 1835. The earlier three-building conjecture is superseded by this record’s phase change_note and the actual Andreas page176. ARCHETYPE CHOICE: outbuilding rather than log_dwelling, and the difference is not cosmetic. log_dwelling reads a storey count, a loft, a chimney count and a sign, and builds a dwelling's openings; outbuilding builds a plain box with one door sized by what has to get through it, and refuses a storey count outright. A jail is the second of those. The archetype's own family list - stable, shed, barn, smokehouse, privy, crib, woodshed - does not name a jail, and that is a gap in the vocabulary rather than a reason to build the wrong thing. WHAT THE PLATE ADDED, 2026-08-21 (T-0070, the owner asking that the jail 'should be made more accurate' to its engraving): a tier-5 pictorial of this building, written up as image 1 of the 2026-08-18 brief, which the record did not have when it was placed. It reached two attributes and no others. The roof came down from the archetype's 32-degree gable default to 12 degrees, because the plate draws a near-flat roof and a convention loses to a picture of the thing itself; the construction note stopped calling hewn-versus-round unattested, because the plate draws squared timber laid horizontally. It reached NEITHER dimension nor coordinate, and it may not: a tier-5 pictorial drives form, materials and massing as inferred and never a footprint outline or a position. So the footprint is still invented and still says so. WHAT WOULD UPGRADE THIS RECORD, in order of value: any county order or contract for the jail's construction, which is the kind of document that carries a dimension, a cost or a specification for the logs; Andreas vol. 1 at page-image level around scan pp. 367 and 1243, which is where the prose transcribed into this project's dossier sits; and the Cook County Sheriff's own published jail history, cited in the dossier and not yet held as a source record here.\n\n1902 PUBLICATION FOUND — T-2380, LATER SAME-DAY SUPERSESSION (2026-10-23). The exact detailed jail artwork has now been actually read in Edwin O. Gale’s 1902Reminiscences of Early Chicago and Vicinity, plate facing printed p53, source trowbridge_first_jail_gale_1902. Actual title credits ILLUSTRATED BY W.E.S.TROWBRIDGE and Fleming H. Revell Company, Chicago/New York/Toronto, MCMII; actual copyright page states 1902 and October. The independently published 1902artwork is public domain by age and unblocks project picture work from that plate. It supersedes the earlier artwork-level hold, without assuming publication from UCR’s 1930 combined date or clearing later copy-specific additions. The UCR catalogue remains check_required/text_only with its required credit retained. No school/Tremont permission is being extended to the jail.\n\nNARRATIVE AND SCENE DATE REMAIN BOUNDED. Gale p25 dates arrival to 25 May 1835; p43 starts the early-morning town stroll; p51 says a small log jail is concealed by a high plank palisade and John Beach occupies a house partly in that enclosure facing Randolph. The courthouse is “just started.” Pages 52–55 continue the stroll and give no jail alteration sequence. This is a 1902 recollection framed in 1835, while the actual plate explicitly says 1836. It supports the jail/keeper-house distinction but does not measure the core or prove every pictured detail existed by 1 July 1835. The independent Andreas hip-roof contrast and separate-oaken-cell question remain. No form, dimensions, source linkage from a form attribute, date, confidence, placement or recipe changes here; earlier numeric geometry proposals remain unapplied pending actual model review. T-2380 stays open.\n\nINDEPENDENT PUBLIC-DOMAIN CONTRAST. The actual Andreas’s 1884 History of Chicago vol.1 pages 175–177 were read. Page 175 distinguishes the first 1833 log jail from a later, separate oaken cell without dating the addition. Page 176 dates the first brick courthouse to FALL 1835, after the target 1 July scene. The plate on page 177, Copyright secured by A.T.Andreas 1884, shows a low house/stockade and a rear log block under a shallow hip/pyramidal cap; the same artwork is explicitly captioned FIRST COURT HOUSE AND JAIL in Andreas’s 1884 History of Cook County page 353. This confirms the jail ensemble, but not which pictured cell is the original 1833 core, its July 1835 appearance or an absolute eave/pitch. It conflicts with the taller gable in the rights-held detailed drawing. The existing model remains a provisional proxy; no roof, height, opening, date, placement or confidence value is altered by this metadata correction. T-2380 remains open.",
