-  "research_note": "PICTURE CONTEXT: The Images tab links kinzie_1857_chicago_1831_panorama, the keyed \u201cFort Dearborn and Chicago in 1831\u201d cut whose caption names \u201cAgency-House.\u201d The verified reproduction is in John Wentworth\u2019s 1881 Fort Dearborn, printed p. 86; its heading credits Kinzie\u2019s Wau-Bun, 1857, without establishing the illustration\u2019s first edition. This is one retrospective panorama, not an independent second witness to Wau-Bun\u2019s written description. Its small agency-house subject supplies context, not measured dimensions, a solved camera, an accepted image match or proof of unchanged survival to 1 July 1835. The building\u2019s centre, wings, tails, partial siding and scene-date survival retain their stated evidence limits.\n\nTHE OLDEST BUILDING STANDING ON THE NORTH BANK IN THIS SCENE, if it was standing: begun under Charles Jouett's agency (1816-18), finished and lived in by Dr. Alexander Wolcott from about 1820, nicknamed Cobweb Castle during his bachelor years, and described once \u2014 in Wau-Bun, of 1831 \u2014 in the sentence that gives this record its shape. TWO SOURCES AGREE ON THE CORNER and neither gives a dimension. WHAT WRITING THIS RECORD ADDED TO THE PROJECT'S DOSSIER, all from Andreas vol. 1 read at full text rather than through the index: the footnote at p. 103 citing Wau-Bun for the corner; McKee's 'the agency-house being afterward clapboarded part way up'; Mrs. Callis's 'a log building of two large rooms, about twenty steps from the river bank, on the north side' for the Jouett-era house; and the 1830 recollection that 'the Government agency-house, known as \"Cobweb Castle,\" was left unoccupied by the death of Dr. Wolcott. In its vicinity were small log buildings occupied by the blacksmith, Mr. McKee, and Billy Caldwell' \u2014 which is the corroboration for Wau-Bun's ring of employees' log residences and for this project's blacksmith_shop_state_st record. THE RECORD IS FLAGGED review_required BECAUSE OF WHAT THIS BUILDING WAS: the United States Indian Agency at Chicago, the administrative seat of the process that ended in the removal of the Potawatomi in August 1835, six weeks after the scene date. AGENTS.md places that subject under a standing constraint: nothing here depicts, stages or narrates it, and any interpretive text hung on this building needs review before a scene carrying it is released. See docs/RESEARCH/cobweb_castle.md.",
