+# Lake House: a dated worksite, with a finite picture ruling
+
+The July 1835 default is an active hotel-building project. The held *Chicago
+Democrat* notice of 10 June 1835, p.2 col.5 (`chicago_democrat_1835_06_10#c001`),
+is the pre-scene evidence. Its transcription-mediated reading reports a new
+house building in Kinzie's Addition, understood to be named Lake House. It does
+not measure the plan or progress. Choosing a bounded construction stage is
+permitted reconstruction under AGENTS.md, **RECONSTRUCTED IS A TIER**, rather
+than an unending prerequisite for a building model.
+
+## The complete known picture set and its scope
+
+The actual images were opened, including the original T-2379 picture, the
+name-collision candidate, and all four Lake House illustrations embedded in the
+live Chicagology source page. The source catalog and Prairie image library were
+searched for Lake House and the held filename. This is coverage of the known
+repository/ticket sources, not a claim to have exhausted all archives.
+
+| Actual image | What the image shows | July 1835 ruling |
+|---|---|---|
+| `chicago/reference/images/chicago/chicago_fort_dearborn_river_mouth_1830.jpg` | The 1856 Kellogg/Blanchard **Old Fort Dearborn** print: the labelled completed Lake House behind the fort at right, with three regular window rows, a completed cornice/roof and central domed cupola; partly covered by the foreground tree and small roof. The existing catalog dates the scene ca.1836–1849. | **Ineligible to prescribe the July construction stage.** Its completed roof, cupola, window rows, façade height and sign are features of its later state. It remains a credited later-context Images link. No assertion that an unfinished July shell matches this finished hotel is made. |
+| `chicago/reference/planning/images/chicago/chicago_fort_dearborn_river_mouth_1830.jpg` | Byte-identical duplicate of the preceding image. | Same composition and ruling; not a second witness or another camera. |
+| `chicago/reference/images/milwaukee/milwaukee_lake_house_hotel_1845.jpg` (and planning duplicate) | Milwaukee's later Lake House: four levels, two-level open gallery, corner cupola, four chimney stacks and steps. | A different building in a different city; excluded by identity. Its gallery, bays and cupola do not govern Chicago's model. |
+| [Chicagology `lakehouse1859.jpg`](https://chicagology.com/wp-content/themes/revolution-20/PreFire/lakehouse1859.jpg) | Photograph captioned by the page “Lake House and Rush Street Bridge #1 / N. Rush and E. Hubbard / About 1860.” Completed large hotel with four apparent window levels, cornice, cupola, several stacks and its later built-up surroundings. Filename 1859 and page caption about 1860 differ; no original repository attribution is supplied there. | **Later completed state**, over twenty years after July 1835. No construction-stage details can be dated backwards. The source remains `check_required` / `cross_check`; no model asset is derived from this photograph. |
+| [1857 advertisement image](https://chicagology.com/wp-content/themes/revolution-20/PreFire3/westerngazetteadvertisement5sep1857lakehouse.jpg) | A text advertisement headed Western Railroad Gazette, September 5, 1857; Lake House, corner Michigan, Kinzie and Rush, George E. Boardman, proprietor. No building drawing. | Later trade/address text, not a picture of architectural form or July progress. Its capacity and rates do not supply July beds, dimensions or work stage. |
+| [1856 advertisement / 1854 menu composite](https://chicagology.com/wp-content/themes/revolution-20/PreFire3/westernrailroadgazetteadvertisement15nov1856lakehouse1854menu.jpg) | Text headed Western Railroad Gazette, November 15, 1856 beside a Lake House dinner bill dated Sunday June 11, 1854. Neither panel depicts the building. | Later trade material only; no architectural or July-stage instruction. The composite has two dates, neither silently treated as the scene date. |
+| [1869 Sanborn map composite](https://chicagology.com/wp-content/themes/revolution-20/PreFire3/lakehouse1869map.jpg) | Page caption “Lake House / Sanborn Fire Insurance Map / 1869.” The composite points from the index to the labelled hotel block **west of Rush**, between the Michigan and Kinzie frontages. The markup and source-sheet history are not independently verified here. | Later mapped hotel, not an 1835 construction plan. The west-side position strengthens the held later west-half-block lead and contradicts the existing reconstructed east-side choice. This run records that conflict rather than silently treating the later footprint or mapped address as July coordinates. No georeference, metric trace or relocation is claimed; the source remains `check_required`. |
+| NYPL/Commons full scan identified by `kellogg_blanchard_1856_old_fort_lake_house` | The same Kellogg/Blanchard print, read directly in the prior credited-source work; not an additional view. The prior source records the NYPL catalog access placeholder and attribution through Commons. | Same later-state limit. This run re-read the held original composition; it does not claim a fresh live NYPL catalog reading. |
+
+Original/planning Chicago image SHA-256:
+`8b0f864e238896900bad6e9429928257c0e9078921a7879bbd4ef48b7317c6a8`.
+Milwaukee image SHA-256:
+`73bec7efb28a851aadc3f618c9050353af084123706787db9c9af90b7f73315d`.
+
+A solved camera would not turn the later completed hotel into evidence of July
+progress. No source camera or `matches` record has been manufactured by assigning
+all its visible architecture an empty “not shown” verdict. The original ticket's
+picture/date assumption has a finite, documented disposition: neither its print
+nor any additional known source-page image depicts an eligible picture of `shell_1835`. Any ticket completion must record that explicit
+wrong-date ruling using the existing image-gate exception, not report a geometric
+match to this later image. A later completed-state model can use the print's form
+within its own dates; it is not necessary to fill the July worksite with a cupola.
+
+## Text and map evidence that bounds the chosen state
+
+- **June 10, 1835 project notice:** active named project before the scene;
+  transcription-mediated, with bracketed supplies. No original newspaper
+  facsimile newly read here. No stage, dimension or measured schedule.
+- **Andreas, 1884, vol.1:** the completed brick hotel, three storeys and a
+  basement, nearly $100,000; autumn 1836 opening; Kinzie/Rush/Michigan streets,
+  fronting Michigan. These are later accounts, not a July progress report.
+- **Bonnell, quoted by Andreas:** “in course of construction” after the arrival
+  dated August 25, 1835 in his letter. Its forty-years arithmetic implies 1836,
+  and Andreas's lead-in says 1837. That disagreement remains declared. It cannot
+  produce a measured July wall height.
+- **Martineau, June 1836, quoted by Andreas:** unfinished hotel interior with
+  benches and planks on trestles. This does not establish July 1835 roof absence.
+- **Fergus, 1839 recalled in 1876, p.37:** the directly checked printed address
+  “Rush and North Water sts”. It disagrees with Andreas's street account. The
+  existing reconstructed position stays on the north-side Michigan/Hubbard
+  interpretation; neither the retrospective print nor the modern Wrigley gloss
+  moves a survey position. The newly read 1869 Sanborn reproduction places the later hotel west of Rush,
+  strengthening that later west-half-block lead. It is not a July construction
+  survey or a hotel-lot deed; no new contemporary July lot plan was recovered.
+- **Chicagology and drloih compilations:** the editorial 1835 groundbreaking is
+  not Andreas's quotation. No independent July stage is supplied. The separate
+  June notice is the stronger project-date evidence.
+- **The earlier bounded 28-issue primary search:** retained as transcription/OCR
+  coverage, not proof of absent notices, and not repeated as a new source reading.
+  The prior dossier records 3,215,983 characters, corpus hashes and the twelve
+  broader false candidates.
+
+The four live source-page images were fetched and read on 2026-11-10. Only
+their date, identity and scope are used here; no geometry or asset is derived
+from the `check_required` site. Their hashes and reading dispositions are in
+`source-coverage.json`. This bounded check is complete; it does not initiate an
+open-ended search for unknown progress pictures.
+
+The full earlier source readings and conflicts remain in
+`docs/RESEARCH/lake_house_construction.md`. The corresponding sources are
+`chicago_democrat_1833_1835`, `andreas_1884_v1`,
+`chicagology_prefire112`, `drloih_hotels`,
+`fergus_chicago_directory_1839`, and
+`kellogg_blanchard_1856_old_fort_lake_house`. No citation was invented or upgraded.
+
+## What now changes in the scene
+
+The earlier model was a uniform four-metre rectangular magazine skin, with one
+painted door surface. The `construction_site` opt-in replaces that with:
+
+- **Real masonry thickness and openings:** 0.46 m walls; seven selected north
+  work bays, with unglazed voids. Only three bays have brickwork bridged overhead, supported by timber lintels
+  bearing 0.22 m into each pier. Lintels are reconstructed too.
+  The central opening starts at grade; this is no claim of an interior walkthrough.
+- **Uneven working lifts:** north-side wall heights of 4.0, 3.28 and 2.44 m;
+  side walls step toward a rear lift of 1.2–2.08 m. A two-metre break in the rear
+  lift allows the worksite to read as material access rather than a closed shed.
+- **Two internal timber working stages:** standards, ledgers and separate deck
+  planks support work at the higher lift. Their 1.65 m decks and all stock and
+  positions are reconstructed.
+- The existing **four brick stacks, three timber piles and two stone heaps**
+  remain the independently declared yard layer. This increment does not invent
+  a supplier transaction or imply they came from a particular yard.
+
+Everything above is **reconstructed**. It fits the existing 24 × 15 m plan, stays
+at or below the existing 4 m maximum, and adds no finished roof, cupola, glazing,
+nameboard, beds, cellar excavation or exact construction-date claim. The salmon
+brick recipe remains the existing separately declared reconstruction. The main
+position and its uncertainty remain unchanged.
+
+## Finite acceptance, before completion is claimed
+
+The street-visible bar is AGENTS.md R11/T-3153: wrong shape or count, missing
+required element, material, or more than about 0.3 m in an eligible source stays
+open. Here no known picture depicts July progress, so no pictured completed
+architecture is silently imposed on July. The chosen worksite has a finite
+geometry and application bar:
+
+1. Native generation uses the pinned producer and hashes this helper when the
+   opt-in is true. Only the explicit roofless brick worksite takes this path;
+   existing fort kinds keep their default behavior.
+2. The final asset shows thick, genuinely open walls with the declared uneven
+   lifts and two supported stages; no uniform cap, dark door patch, finished
+   hotel roof or cupola appears. Pieces remain within 24 × 15 × 4 m.
+3. The published July scene selects `shell_1835`. Desktop and 390×780 inspection
+   shows the site grounded beside its existing stock, a readable exterior and
+   the card's declared liberties, with no page errors.
+4. Final exact-input render/asset hashes and actual published review results
+   are recorded by the integration run. Source-only checks are not reported as
+   those rendered results. The image exception, if used, states the wrong-date
+   reason instead of claiming `matches`.
+
+Local source checks: schema PASS; helper syntax PASS; the **actual Lake House
+record → the integration `from_phase` → this helper** emits 65 positive-volume
+solid pieces inside 24 × 15 × 4 m. Every emitted piece has `_CONFIDENCE` input
+1.0: the existing GLB contract assigns attested 0.0, inferred 0.5 and reconstructed
+1.0. The building's attested project existence does not promote its invented
+walls or stages. A CPU preview was opened and inspected. The global gate,
+native bake and final published visual acceptance were **not run in this source
+checkpoint**; they are required on the integrated exact inputs. Before-master SHA-256:
+`c9002baa0a01b6a166c26c78178fb3000f4a0ec453d6088c675f11774cbd97d0`.
